      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the distance between the vertices of the hyperbola given by the equation \\(\\frac{y^2}{27} - \\frac{x^2}{11} = 1\\), we need to understand the standard form of the hyperbola equation and its properties. The standard form of a hyperbola that opens up and down (with the y-axis as the transverse axis) is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{y^2}{a^2} - \\frac{x^2}{b^2} = 1\n\\]\n\nFrom this equation, we can see that:\n- \\(a^2 = 27\\) (this gives \\(a = \\sqrt{27} = 3\\sqrt{3}\\))\n- \\(b^2 = 11\\) (this gives \\(b = \\sqrt{11}\\))\n\nThe distance between the vertices of a hyperbola when it opens up and down is \\(2a\\). So, \\(2a = 2 \\cdot 3\\sqrt{3} = 6\\sqrt{3}\\).\n\nLet's use Python to verify this result. We'll calculate the square root values and compute the final distance.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Given values\na_squared = 27\nb_squared = 11\n\n# Calculate a and b\na = sp.sqrt(a_squared)\nb = sp.sqrt(b_squared)\n\n# Distance between the vertices\ndistance_between_vertices = 2 * a\nprint(distance_between_vertices)\n````\n6*sqrt(3)\n```\nThe distance between the vertices of the hyperbola \\(\\frac{y^2}{27} - \\frac{x^2}{11} = 1\\) is \\(\\boxed{6\\sqrt{3}}\\).",
