      "text": "To find the distance between the vertices of the hyperbola \\(\\frac{y^2}{27} - \\frac{x^2}{11} = 1\\), we start by identifying the parameters \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) from the standard form of the hyperbola equation \\(\\frac{y^2}{a^2} - \\frac{x^2}{b^2} = 1\\).\n\nGiven:\n\\[\n\\frac{y^2}{27} - \\frac{x^2}{11} = 1\n\\]\nwe can directly see that:\n\\[\na^2 = 27 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b^2 = 11.\n\\]\nTherefore,\n\\[\na = \\sqrt{27} = 3\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nb = \\sqrt{11}.\n\\]\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola are located at \\((0, \\pm a)\\). Thus, the vertices are:\n\\[\n(0, 3\\sqrt{3}) \\quad \\text{and} \\quad (0, -3\\sqrt{3}).\n\\]\n\nTo find the distance \\(d\\) between these vertices:\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{(3\\sqrt{3} - (-3\\sqrt{3}))^2 + 0^2} = \\sqrt{(3\\sqrt{3} + 3\\sqrt{3})^2} = \\sqrt{(6\\sqrt{3})^2} = \\sqrt{108} = 6\\sqrt{3}.\n\\]\n\nLet us confirm this calculation with Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Calculate the distance d between the vertices of the hyperbola\na = math.sqrt(27)\nb = math.sqrt(11)\nd = math.sqrt((a - (-a))**2)\n\nprint(d)\n```\n```output\n10.392304845413264\n```\nThe distance \\(d\\) between the vertices of the hyperbola is \\(\\boxed{6\\sqrt{3}}\\), which is approximately \\(10.392\\).",
